<h2>What Hail Damages on Dallas Properties</h2>
<p>Hail is a named peril on most homeowners and commercial property policies, subject to deductibles, exclusions, and endorsements. Claims here commonly involve:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Roofs.</strong> Composition shingles on brick-veneer homes (granule loss, bruising, cracked mats), plus metal, tile, and slate on some houses, and TPO or modified-bitumen membranes on flat and low-slope commercial roofs. See our <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a> page.</li>
<li><strong>Windows and skylights.</strong> Broken glass, cracked frames, and shredded screens.</li>
<li><strong>Siding and trim.</strong> Split or dented siding, chipped paint, damaged fascia and soffits. Brick usually holds up, but wood trim, window wraps, and sealants around it may not.</li>
<li><strong>Soft metals and equipment.</strong> Gutters, downspouts, roof vents, flashing, and the condenser coil fins on AC units.</li>
<li><strong>Other structures.</strong> Fences, sheds, pergolas, and detached garages.</li>
<li><strong>Interior water damage</strong> from broken windows or punctured roofing, usually handled as part of the same claim.</li>
</ul>

